Functions
*********

.[perex]
The `functions` section decides on functions and closures (anonymous functions): whether a closure that only returns one expression is written as an arrow function, whether a closure that does not use `$this` gets `static`, whether `true` and `false` are passed as named arguments, and what to do with a `return` at the end of a function, with a parameter default that is never used, and with a function declared inside another function.

How a signature and its parentheses are written belongs to other sections: the [spacing |spacing] section covers the space after `function` and `fn`, the [braces |braces] section covers the brace of a closure, and the [types |types] section covers the types of parameters.

```neon
functions:
	arrowFunction:
		oneExpression: required
	trailingBareReturn: forbidden
	unreachableParameterDefault: forbidden
```

```php .[before]
$names = array_map(function ($user) {  // The closure returning a single expression must be written as an arrow function.
	return $user->name;
}, $users);

function writeLog(string $message, $level = 'info', $context)  // Useless default value of `$level`, because a required parameter follows.
{
	echo $message;
	return;  // Useless return, because the function ends right after it.
}
```

```php .[after]
$names = array_map(fn($user) => $user->name, $users);

function writeLog(string $message, $level, $context)
{
	echo $message;
}
```

A closure is rewritten to an arrow function only where the two mean the same: the closure does not capture a variable by reference, it does not touch a variable it does not name, and no comment is lost.


functions.trailingBareReturn
============================

`return;` as the last statement.

- `forbidden`: never there
- `keep`: nothing is enforced, the code stays as it is

Default `keep` · standards: perCs `keep`, psr12 `keep`, nette `forbidden`, symfony `forbidden`, laravel `forbidden` · covers `no_useless_return` .[decision-facts]


functions.arrowFunction.oneExpression
=====================================

An arrow function written for a closure whose body is a single `return`, where that is equivalent: no variable captured by reference, none reached by a name it does not spell out, no comment lost.

Not about the `static` of the arrow function, which `functions.staticClosure` decides.

- `required`: always there
- `keep`: nothing is enforced, the code stays as it is

Default `keep` · standards: perCs `keep`, psr12 `keep`, nette `required`, symfony `keep`, laravel `keep` · covers `use_arrow_functions`, `SlevomatCodingStandard.Functions.RequireArrowFunction` .[decision-facts]


functions.arrowFunction.emptyBody
=================================

`fn() => null` written for a closure with an empty body, one returning `void`, `never` or by reference aside.

- `required`: always there
- `keep`: nothing is enforced, the code stays as it is

Default `keep` · standards: perCs `required`, psr12 `keep`, nette `keep`, symfony `required`, laravel `keep` .[decision-facts]


functions.arrowFunctionNested
=============================

Whether a closure holding another closure or arrow function becomes one too.

- `yes` or `no`

Default `no` · standards: perCs `no`, psr12 `no`, nette `yes`, symfony `no`, laravel `no` · covers `SlevomatCodingStandard.Functions.RequireArrowFunction` .[decision-facts]


functions.namedBooleanArgument
==============================

A `true` or `false` passed by position to a parameter declared `bool`, written with the name of the parameter, `json_decode($json, associative: true)`; not where a positional argument follows it, nor in a call of a setter, `setVisible(true)`, or of a function PHP calls without a frame or optimizes.

- `required`: always there
- `keep`: nothing is enforced, the code stays as it is

Default `keep` · standards: perCs `keep`, psr12 `keep`, nette `keep`, symfony `keep`, laravel `keep` .[decision-facts]

A named argument says what the `true` or `false` turns on or off, so the reader does not have to look up the signature. DressCode names only arguments at the end of a call, with no positional argument after them, because PHP does not accept a positional argument after a named one. A setter like `setVisible(true)` already says this in its name, so it stays as it is. Functions that PHP calls without a stack frame, or that the compiler optimizes, such as `in_array()`, also stay as they are: a named argument would cost them the optimization. You can list other functions and methods in `functions.namedBooleanArgumentExcept`.

DressCode knows the parameter names of PHP functions by itself. For methods, `new` and the functions of your project, it needs [the types of the code |/types]; without them, it leaves such calls alone.

```neon
functions:
	namedBooleanArgument: required
	namedBooleanArgumentExcept: [print_r]
```

```php .[before]
$data = json_decode($json, true);  // The boolean argument of `json_decode()` must be named `associative`.
$html = htmlspecialchars($text, ENT_QUOTES, 'UTF-8', false);  // The boolean argument of `htmlspecialchars()` must be named `double_encode`.
echo print_r($data, true);
```

```php .[after]
$data = json_decode($json, associative: true);
$html = htmlspecialchars($text, ENT_QUOTES, 'UTF-8', double_encode: false);
echo print_r($data, true);
```

The fix is a risky fix for a call to a method that a child class can override, such as `$this->render(true)` in a class that is not final, or a call through an interface: the child class can give the parameter a different name, and the call would then fail. Calls to a `private` or `final` method, calls to a method of a final class, calls through `self::` and `parent::`, and `new` are not risky. Risky fixes are made once you list the decision in the [`fixRisky` |/configuration#risky-fixes] key.


functions.namedBooleanArgumentExcept
====================================

The functions and methods whose arguments stay positional, a method written with the class declaring it or the one it is called on, `Acme\Shop\Cart::add`.

- a list of names

Default `[]` · standards: perCs `[]`, psr12 `[]`, nette `[]`, symfony `[]`, laravel `[]` .[decision-facts]


functions.innerFunction
=======================

A named function declared inside a function, a method, a closure or a property hook, which is only reported.

- `forbidden`: never there
- `keep`: nothing is enforced, the code stays as it is

Default `keep` · standards: perCs `keep`, psr12 `keep`, nette `forbidden`, symfony `keep`, laravel `keep` · covers `Squiz.PHP.InnerFunctions` .[decision-facts]


functions.staticClosure
=======================

The `static` keyword of a closure or an arrow function that does not use `$this`.

- `required`: always there, `static function`
- `keep`: nothing is enforced, the code stays as it is

Default `keep` · standards: perCs `keep`, psr12 `keep`, nette `keep`, symfony `keep`, laravel `keep` · covers `static_lambda`, `SlevomatCodingStandard.Functions.StaticClosure` .[decision-facts]

The fix is a risky fix: a closure can be bound to an object somewhere else, through `bindTo()` or `Closure::bind()`, and that cannot be seen from a single file. The fix is made once you list the decision in the [`fixRisky` |/configuration#risky-fixes] key.


functions.unreachableParameterDefault
=====================================

A default a required parameter after it makes unreachable.

- `forbidden`: never there
- `keep`: nothing is enforced, the code stays as it is

Default `keep` · standards: perCs `keep`, psr12 `keep`, nette `forbidden`, symfony `keep`, laravel `forbidden` · covers `no_unreachable_default_argument_value`, `PEAR.Functions.ValidDefaultValue`, `SlevomatCodingStandard.Functions.UselessParameterDefaultValue` .[decision-facts]
